{"name":"Oxford Street","hex":"#bda07f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"oxford","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Oxford (cloth)","Alternative letter-case form of Oxford (shoe)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/oxford"},{"term":"street","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A paved part of road, usually in a village or a town.","A road as above, but including the sidewalks (pavements) and buildings."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To build or equip with streets.","To eject; to throw onto the streets."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/street"}],"entries":[{"title":"Oxford Street","blocks":[{"text":"Oxford Street is a major road in the City of Westminster in the West End of London, running between Marble Arch and Tottenham Court Road via Oxford Circus. It marks the notional boundary between the areas of Fitzrovia and Marylebone to the north, with Soho and Mayfair to its immediate south. It is Europe's busiest shopping street, with around 300,000 daily visitors, and as of 2012 had approximately 300 shops. It is designated as part of the A40, a major road between London and Fishguard, though it is not signed as such, and traffic is regularly restricted to buses and taxis."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Oxford_Street_%28geograph_4949395%29.jpg/330px-Oxford_Street_%28geograph_4949395%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oxford_Street","wikidata":"Q209714"},{"title":"Oxford","blocks":[{"text":"Oxford is a cathedral city and non-metropolitan district in Oxfordshire, England, of which it is the county town."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Museum_of_Oxford_%285652685943%29.jpg/330px-Museum_of_Oxford_%285652685943%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oxford","wikidata":"Q34217"},{"title":"Street","blocks":[{"text":"A street is a public thoroughfare in a city, town or village, typically lined with buildings on one or both sides. Streets often include pavements (sidewalks), pedestrian crossings, and sometimes amenities like streetlights or benches. A street can be as simple as a level patch of dirt, but is more often paved with a hard, durable surface such as tarmac, concrete, cobblestone or brick. It can be designed for both social activity and movement."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Typical_Street_In_The_Royal_Borough_Of_Kensington_And_Chelsea_In_London.jpg/330px-Typical_Street_In_The_Royal_Borough_Of_Kensington_And_Chelsea_In_London.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Street","wikidata":"Q79007"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/LondonBeforeHouses.jpg/500px-LondonBeforeHouses.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/LondonBeforeHouses.jpg/500px-LondonBeforeHouses.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALondonBeforeHouses.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2f/WyattPantheonOxfordSt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2f/WyattPantheonOxfordSt.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Oxford Street in the late 18th century (with the Pantheon on the left)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWyattPantheonOxfordSt.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Oxford_Street_1875.jpg/330px-Oxford_Street_1875.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campa